{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices across the whole 30-day horizon (often by ~4–10 EUR). Apply a downward level shift to the entire forecast path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when history shows a rollover and emerging decline, the model adjusts too slowly and keeps prices near prior highs. Increase sensitivity to recent negative momentum (e.g., overweight last 5–10 days) to pull the path down faster.",
    "Overestimates rebounds: after sharp drops, the model predicts quick recoveries back toward ~75–80, but realized prices stay depressed (~68–72). Damp rebound amplitude and speed; enforce slower mean reversion after sell-offs.",
    "Excessive smoothness / volatility mismatch: model path is smoother and higher, missing the depth of drawdowns. Add a drawdown extension heuristic (if recent drop >X, extend weakness for Y days before reverting).",
    "Horizon effect: bias remains material even at 20–30 days (not just near-term), implying a structural level error rather than only timing. Use a horizon-wide calibration factor (multiplicative or additive) rather than only short-horizon tweaks."
  ]
}
